  "text": "None of the three answers can be verified, and each argument has a visible gap.\n\n- **Solution 1 (q−1):** The claimed relation at weight q, involving ζ(q) and ζ(q−1,1), is asserted rather than derived. Depth-one values satisfy ζ(s)^q = ζ(qs), so ζ(q) = ζ(1)^q is not an F_q-linear relation with the other weight-q values. The Carlitz–Bernoulli argument also doesn't produce an F_q-linear relation.\n\n- **Solution 2 (q+2):** The valuation computation is concrete and checkable. The coincidence V(q+2,1) = V(1,1,q+1) = q²+2q is correct arithmetic. But distinct leading valuations are only sufficient for independence, and the claim that a relation exists at weight q+3 is stated as \"known\" without a source. The argument does not establish the threshold.\n\n- **Solution 3 (q):** The recurrence d(w) = d(w−1) + d(w−q) is attributed to Todd without support, and it is not clear it counts F_q-linear rather than F_q(T)-linear dimension. The q=2 check uses this recurrence, so it inherits the same doubt.\n\nI can't confirm the threshold from memory. I believe the literature on Thakur's conjecture (work by Im, Kim, Le, Ngo Dac and Pham) exists, but I can't recall the exact weight where F_q-linear relations first appear, and none of the three proposed expressions is established by its argument. So I can't identify a correct solution.\n\nCHOICE: NONE",
